---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const dashboard = 'https://hridal.github.io/portfolio-risk-dashboard/';
const marketWatch = dashboard + 'market.html';
---
<Base title="Portfolio risk dashboard" description="A dashboard Hridal built to understand how investments behave before putting her own money in, which checks its own data and updates itself every weekday.">
  <article class="case">
    <a class="case-back" href="/projects">All projects</a>
    <p class="case-kind">Personal project, October 2026 to now</p>
    <h1>Portfolio risk dashboard</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">A dashboard I built before I started investing, to see how the portfolios I was considering behave when markets go wrong and to make decisions I can check later. It rebuilds itself every weekday evening.</p>

    <figure class="case-viewer">
      <div class="viewer-box app-box" id="dashbox">
        <div class="app-poster">
          <p class="app-name">Portfolio risk dashboard</p>
          <p class="app-sub">Live, updated every weekday evening.</p>
          <button class="btn solid" id="load-dashboard">Open the dashboard here</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The portfolios are ones I'm testing, not recommendations. <a href={dashboard} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it in a new tab</a>, or see <a href={marketWatch} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Market Watch</a>, the page that follows US stocks.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="case-facts">
      <div><dt>What it covers</dt><dd>Four portfolios of UK-listed funds, from 2012 to today</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Tools</dt><dd>Python, pandas, matplotlib, GitHub Actions, and Finnhub for Market Watch</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Data</dt><dd>Daily fund prices, cross-checked against a second source, and UK inflation from the ONS</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Runs</dt><dd>Automatically every weekday after the London and US closes, with checks before anything is published</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>Before putting any money in, I didn't want to choose a portfolio from a headline return. I wanted to know how far each option falls in a crash and how long it takes to recover, what fees and inflation really cost over twenty years, and whether investing a lump sum or spreading it out makes more difference than people think.</p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <p>The dashboard compares four portfolios, each starting with £10,000. For each one it shows the return, the volatility, the worst fall from a previous high, the worst month and year, and the return after inflation. It replays every portfolio through real market shocks, from the Brexit vote and the Covid crash to the 2022 bond sell-off and the UK gilt crisis. It also works out what fees cost over twenty years, whether a lump sum would have beaten investing monthly for every start month since 2012, and the realistic range of outcomes for saving £200 a month.</p>

    <h2>Getting the numbers right</h2>
    <p>Free price data is messy, and a dashboard that quietly shows wrong numbers is worse than none. Every run goes through a set of checks before anything is published. They catch London prices that switch between pence and pounds, missing days, prices that stop updating and moves too large to be real, and they compare every price against a second, independent source. Nothing is fixed without being logged, and if a check fails the page says its figures may be wrong rather than hiding it. Serious problems open a ticket that I close once I've dealt with them.</p>

    <h2>Making better decisions</h2>
    <p>Every investing decision goes into a journal with my reasoning at the time and a date to look back at it, and the dashboard shows what has happened since. Writing the reasoning down first is the only way I know to tell a good decision from a lucky one. Once I start investing, the dashboard will track my real portfolio too, including how much my own timing helps or hurts.</p>

    <h2>Market Watch</h2>
    <p>I also wanted a way to keep up with the market without reacting to every headline, so I added a second page that follows a watchlist of US stocks. It shows how each one has done over the last day to the last year next to the S&amp;P 500, and flags moves that were unusual for that stock, splitting each one into what the market and its sector explain and what looks specific to the company. It also gathers the headlines from around each move, so I can see what was in the news at the time.</p>
    <p>For each stock it also shows separate signals, such as momentum, the trend, analyst ratings and the next earnings date, each with what it can't tell me. There's no combined score and no price prediction. Every run logs what the signals said and checks a month later whether they were right, so I can find out whether they deserve any weight.</p>
    <p><a class="btn" href={marketWatch} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Market Watch</a></p>

    <h2>What's next</h2>
    <p>I'm adding a macro panel with the Bank of England base rate, gilt yields and inflation alongside the portfolios, and a breakdown of what each fund is really exposed to.</p>
  </article>
  <script>
    document.getElementById('load-dashboard')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const box = document.getElementById('dashbox')!;
      const f = document.createElement('iframe');
      f.src = 'https://hridal.github.io/portfolio-risk-dashboard/'; f.title = 'Portfolio risk dashboard';
      box.replaceChildren(f);
    });
  </script>
</Base>
